2026-01-14  \\n**Executive Director:** Orchestrating implementation\\n\\n## Vision\\n\\nCreate a humorous Steam-style achievements system that awards different agent classes tongue-in-cheek achievements based on their actions. The system should track agent behavior and award achievements as milestones are reached, adding a playful gamification layer to the ensemble agent system.\\n\\n## Objectives\\n\\n1. **Achievement Definition System**: Create a flexible schema for defining achievements with:\\n   - Achievement name (humorous/creative)\\n   - Description\\n   - Target agent class(es)\\n   - Trigger conditions\\n   - Icon/badge representation\\n   - Rarity tier (common, rare, epic, legendary)\\n\\n2. **Achievement Tracking**: Monitor agent actions and automatically award achievements when conditions are met\\n\\n3. **Achievement Display**: Present awarded achievements in the UI with visual feedback (toast notifications, achievement card displays)\\n\\n4. **Achievement History**: Maintain a persistent record of all achievements earned by agent class\\n\\n5. **Achievement Categories**: Organize achievements by theme:\\n   - Productivity (tasks completed, speed records)\\n   - Comedy (failures, quirky behaviors)\\n   - Milestones (first-time events)\\n   - Streaks (consecutive actions)\\n   - Meta (self-referential humor about being an AI)\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n### In Scope\\n\\n**Backend Components:**\\n- Achievement data model and schema\\n- Achievement definition storage (JSON/database)\\n- Achievement tracking service that monitors agent activity\\n- Achievement award logic and evaluation engine\\n- API endpoints for:\\n  - Fetching available achievements\\n  - Fetching awarded achievements (by agent class, by project)\\n  - Triggering achievement evaluation\\n  - Achievement statistics\\n\\n**Frontend Components:**\\n- Achievement notification system (toast/banner when earned)\\n- Achievement gallery/showcase view\\n- Achievement progress indicators (for multi-step achievements)\\n- Agent profile page showing earned achievements\\n- Achievement filtering and sorting\\n\\n**Achievement Content:**\\n- 20-30 humorous achievements per major agent class:\\n  - Executive Director\\n  - Development Manager\\n  - System Architect\\n  - Code Writer\\n  - Code Tester\\n  - Bug Fixer\\n  - Documentation Writer\\n- Examples:\\n  - \\"Executive Overreach\\" - Executive Director tries to write code (blocked by permissions)\\n  - \\"Infinite Recursion Champion\\" - Spawned 10 sub-agents in a single task\\n  - \\"Test Whisperer\\" - Code Tester achieves 100% test coverage\\n  - \\"Bug Whisperer\\" - Bug Fixer fixes 50 bugs without creating new ones\\n  - \\"RTFM Generator\\" - Documentation Writer creates docs longer than the code\\n  - \\"Premature Optimization\\" - Code Writer refactors before requirements are clear\\n  - \\"Merge Conflict Survivor\\" - Successfully resolved 10 Git conflicts\\n  - \\"Commit Message Poet\\" - 10 commit messages >100 characters\\n  - \\"TDD Purist\\" - Wrote tests before code 100 times\\n\\n### Out of Scope\\n\\n- User-created custom achievements (Phase 2 feature)\\n- Achievement leaderboards across different ensemble instances\\n- Achievement trading or social features\\n- Blockchain/NFT integration for achievements\\n- Achievements that affect agent behavior or permissions\\n- Multiplayer/competitive achievement mechanics\\n- External platform integration (actual Steam, Discord badges, etc.)\\n\\n## User Personas\\n\\n**Primary User: Developer/Operator**\\n- Monitors ensemble agent activity\\n- Enjoys gamification and humor in development tools\\n- Appreciates insights into agent behavior patterns\\n- Values both functionality and entertainment\\n\\n**Secondary User: Executive Director (Self-Aware AI)**\\n- Tracks performance metrics indirectly through achievements\\n- Gains insight into which agents are most active\\n- Uses achievement patterns to identify optimization opportunities\\n\\n## Features\\n\\n### F1: Achievement Definition System\\n- **Priority:** High\\n- **Description:** JSON-based achievement schema with:\\n  ```json\\n  {\\n    \\"id\\": \\"executive_overreach\\",\\n    \\"name\\": \\"Executive Overreach\\",\\n    \\"description\\": \\"Attempted to write code despite lacking can_write_code permission\\",\\n    \\"agent_class\\": \\"executive_director\\",\\n    \\"category\\": \\"comedy\\",\\n    \\"rarity\\": \\"common\\",\\n    \\"trigger\\": {\\n      \\"event_type\\": \\"permission_denied\\",\\n      \\"action\\": \\"write_code_file\\"\\n    },\\n    \\"icon\\": \\"\\ud83d\\udeab\\ud83d\\udcbb\\",\\n    \\"points\\": 10\\n  }\\n  ```\\n\\n### F2: Real-Time Achievement Tracking\\n- **Priority:** High\\n- **Description:** Service that monitors agent activity logs and evaluates achievement conditions\\n- Integrates with existing activity tracker\\n- Triggers award ceremony when conditions met\\n\\n### F3: Achievement Notification UI\\n- **Priority:** High\\n- **Description:** Toast notification appears when achievement earned\\n- Shows achievement name, description, icon, rarity\\n- Plays subtle animation/sound effect\\n- Links to full achievement details\\n\\n### F4: Achievement Gallery\\n- **Priority:** Medium\\n- **Description:** Dedicated page showing:\\n  - All available achievements (locked/unlocked state)\\n  - Filter by agent class, category, rarity\\n  - Progress bars for multi-step achievements\\n  - Statistics (total earned, completion percentage)\\n\\n### F5: Agent Profile Achievements\\n- **Priority:** Medium\\n- **Description:** Each agent class profile shows:\\n  - Recently earned achievements\\n  - Rarest achievements\\n  - Achievement count and total points\\n  - Links to full gallery filtered by that agent\\n\\n### F6: Achievement Persistence\\n- **Priority:** High\\n- **Description:** Store achievement awards in database/JSON file\\n- Persist across sessions\\n- Track timestamps of when achieved\\n- Support for achievement history/timeline\\n\\n## Technical Constraints\\n\\n1. **Integration:** Must integrate with existing:\\n   - Activity tracking system (`activity_tracker.py`)\\n   - Agent spawn/execution pipeline\\n   - Project tracking system\\n   - Frontend React components\\n\\n2. **Performance:** Achievement evaluation should not slow down agent execution\\n   - Async/background processing\\n   - Batch evaluation where possible\\n\\n3. **Storage:** Use existing storage patterns:\\n   - JSON files in `~/.ensemble/achievements/`\\n   - Or extend existing SQLite/database if present\\n\\n4. **Technology Stack:**\\n   - Backend: Python/FastAPI (existing)\\n   - Frontend: React + TypeScript (existing)\\n   - Storage: JSON or SQLite\\n   - Real-time updates: WebSocket or polling\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n\\n1. **Functional:**\\n   - \\u2705 20+ achievements defined per major agent class\\n   - \\u2705 Achievements automatically awarded when conditions met\\n   - \\u2705 Notifications appear within 2 seconds of earning\\n   - \\u2705 Achievement history persists across sessions\\n   - \\u2705 Gallery displays all achievements with correct locked/unlocked state\\n\\n2. **User Experience:**\\n   - \\u2705 Achievements are genuinely funny/entertaining\\n   - \\u2705 Notifications are non-intrusive but noticeable\\n   - \\u2705 Achievement progress is clear and motivating\\n   - \\u2705 UI integrates seamlessly with existing interface\\n\\n3. **Technical:**\\n   - \\u2705 No performance degradation in agent execution\\n   - \\u2705 Achievement data persists reliably\\n   - \\u2705 Code follows existing project patterns\\n   - \\u2705 Unit tests cover achievement logic (>80% coverage)\\n   - \\u2705 Integration tests verify end-to-end flow\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n1. **Humor Tone:** Achievements should be lighthearted and self-aware, poking fun at AI/developer culture without being mean-spirited\\n\\n2. **Privacy:** Achievement data is local to the ensemble instance (no external reporting)\\n\\n3. **Extensibility:** System should be designed for easy addition of new achievements in the future\\n\\n4. **Performance:** Existing activity tracking provides sufficient event data for achievement triggers\\n\\n5. **UI Framework:** React frontend exists with component library/patterns to follow\\n\\n6. **Backend API:** FastAPI backend exists with established patterns for new endpoints\\n\\n7. **User Preferences:** Users can disable achievement notifications if desired (settings toggle)\\n\\n## Non-Functional Requirements\\n\\n1. **Maintainability:** Achievement definitions should be easy to add/modify without code changes\\n2. **Testability:** Achievement trigger logic should be unit-testable in isolation\\n3. **Scalability:** System should handle 100+ achievements without performance issues\\n4. **Accessibility:** Achievement UI should be screen-reader friendly\\n5. **Localization:** Initial English-only, but structure should support i18n in future\\n\\n## Risks & Mitigations\\n\\n| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\\n|------|--------|------------|\\n| Achievement evaluation slows agent execution | High | Use async background processing, batch evaluation |\\n| Activity tracker doesn\'t capture needed events | Medium | Extend activity tracker to emit additional events |\\n| Notification fatigue (too many achievements) | Medium | Implement cooldown periods, configurable frequency |\\n| Achievement definitions become stale/outdated | Low | Regular review process, community suggestions |\\n| Storage conflicts with existing data | Medium | Use isolated namespace/directory for achievements |\\n\\n## Milestones\\n\\n1. **M1: Requirements & Architecture** (Current)\\n   - Requirements document complete\\n   - Architecture design approved\\n   - Achievement content brainstormed\\n\\n2. **M2: Backend Implementation**\\n   - Achievement data model\\n   - Tracking service\\n   - API endpoints\\n   - Integration with activity tracker\\n\\n3. **M3: Frontend Implementation**\\n   - Notification component\\n   - Achievement gallery\\n   - Agent profile integration\\n   - Settings/preferences\\n\\n4. **M4: Content & Testing**\\n   - All achievement definitions created\\n   - Unit tests (>80% coverage)\\n   - Integration tests\\n   - Manual QA\\n\\n5. **M5: Deployment & Documentation**\\n   - User documentation\\n   - Developer documentation (adding new achievements)\\n   - Deployment to production\\n   - Launch celebration achievement \\ud83c\\udf89\\n\\n## Open Questions\\n\\nNone - proceeding with assumptions documented above. Executive Director has made decisive choices for:\\n- Technology stack (existing Python/React)\\n- Storage approach (JSON files in ~/.ensemble/achievements/)\\n- Achievement count (20-30 per agent class)\\n- Notification mechanism (toast notifications)\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Status:** Requirements Complete - Ready for Architecture Phase  \\n**Next Step:** Spawn Development Manager with this requirements document\\n"}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_01Av7TEUkPyK3RBceRtwAab8', 'name': 'project_tracking', 'input': {'action': 'add_note', 'project_id': '6df9d558', 'note': 'Found existing achievements system project. User wants to expand with 100 new achievements in musical competition categories: drum and bugle corps (DCI) and British-style brass bands (NABBA)', 'category': 'general'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_01Av7TEUkPyK3RBceRtwAab8', 'content': '{"success": true, "message": "Added note to project"}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_017wgaZsdBY6hHJCS5n7sryW', 'name': 'write_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md', 'content': '# Achievement System Expansion - Requirements Document\n\n## Project Overview\n\n**Project Name:** Achievement System Expansion  \n**Project ID:** 6df9d558  \n**Created:** 2026-01-14  \n**Executive Director:** Orchestrating implementation\n\n## Vision\n\nExpand the existing achievements system by adding 100 new achievements organized into two specialized musical competition categories:\n1. **Drum and Bugle Corps (DCI)** - Achievements themed around competitive marching music (dci.org)\n2. **British-Style Brass Bands (NABBA)** - Achievements themed around British brass band competitions in the USA (nabba.org)\n\nThis expansion will add musical sophistication and specialized humor to the existing achievement system while maintaining the tongue-in-cheek style and agent behavior tracking.\n\n## Objectives\n\n1. **New Achievement Categories**: Add two major new categories to the existing system:\n   - DCI (Drum and Bugle Corps) category with 50 achievements\n   - NABBA (British-Style Brass Bands) category with 50 achievements\n\n2. **Musical Theme Integration**: Create achievements that cleverly blend musical competition terminology with software development and AI agent behaviors\n\n3. **Expanded Tracking**: Extend achievement triggers to recognize new patterns in agent behavior that align with musical competition themes\n\n4. **Category Organization**: Enhance the existing achievement system to support better categorization and filtering\n\n## Scope\n\n### In Scope\n\n**New Achievement Categories:**\n\n**DCI (Drum and Bugle Corps) Achievements (50 total):**\n- Performance-based achievements using DCI terminology\n- Corps competition references (Blue Devils, Santa Clara Vanguard, etc.)\n- Musical competition scoring and judging themes\n- Marching and precision execution metaphors\n- Examples:\n  - "Blue Devils Precision" - Executed 100 tasks with zero errors\n  - "Santa Clara Vanguard" - Led the ensemble in successful deployments \n  - "Perfect Score (99.000)" - Achieved maximum test coverage on first try\n  - "Drum Major Leadership" - Successfully coordinated 5+ agents simultaneously\n  - "Championship Season" - Completed 10 projects without major issues\n  - "Finals Night" - Delivered critical project under extreme deadline pressure\n  - "Hornline Excellence" - Code Writer produced bug-free code 25 times in a row\n  - "Drumline Precision" - Test suite executed with perfect timing 50 times\n  - "Color Guard Grace" - Bug Fixer resolved issues with elegant solutions\n  - "Show Design Genius" - System Architect created flawless architecture on first try\n\n**NABBA (British-Style Brass Bands) Achievements (50 total):**\n- British brass band terminology and tradition\n- Contest and competition references\n- Musical ensemble and conductor themes\n- British cultural references and terminology\n- Examples:\n  - "Championship Section" - Achieved highest performance tier in all metrics\n  - "First Section Rising" - Improved from previous performance significantly  \n  - "Contest March Mastery" - Led successful project deployments like a contest march\n  - "Principal Cornet" - Code Writer took lead on most challenging implementations\n  - "Soprano Cornet Brilliance" - Delivered high-quality code with exceptional clarity\n  - "Euphonium Excellence" - Provided steady, reliable performance across all tasks\n  - "Tuba Foundation" - System Architect provided rock-solid system foundations\n  - "Percussion Section" - Test suite provided perfect rhythmic verification\n  - "Conductor\'s Baton" - Executive Director orchestrated flawless multi-agent coordination\n  - "Adjudicator\'s Approval" - Code review received perfect score from Bug Fixer\n\n**Enhanced Achievement System:**\n- Category filtering and organization improvements\n- Musical terminology integration in existing UI\n- Rarity distribution across both new categories (common, rare, epic, legendary)\n- Progressive achievement chains within each musical theme\n\n### Out of Scope\n\n- Modification of existing achievement definitions (maintaining backward compatibility)\n- Audio/sound integration (musical themes remain textual/visual)\n- External integration with actual DCI or NABBA systems\n- Real-time scoring systems mimicking actual competition scoring\n- Geographic or regional achievement variations\n\n## User Personas\n\n**Primary User: Music-Aware Developer/Operator**\n- Appreciates both software development and musical performance culture\n- Enjoys sophisticated humor that bridges technical and artistic domains\n- Values achievements that recognize both precision and creativity\n- Understands competitive music terminology and culture\n\n**Secondary User: General Ensemble User**\n- May not know musical terminology but can appreciate the precision/performance metaphors\n- Benefits from expanded achievement variety and themed categories\n- Enjoys discovering new achievement themes and terminology\n\n## Features\n\n### F1: DCI Achievement Category\n- **Priority:** High\n- **Description:** 50 achievements themed around drum and bugle corps competitions\n- Musical competition terminology (finals, semifinals, championships)\n- Corps-specific references and inside knowledge\n- Performance scoring and judging metaphors\n- Precision and execution themes\n\n### F2: NABBA Achievement Category  \n- **Priority:** High\n- **Description:** 50 achievements themed around British-style brass band competitions\n- British musical terminology and traditions\n- Contest section levels (Championship, First, Second, etc.)\n- Conductor and ensemble leadership themes\n- Traditional brass band instrumentation references\n\n### F3: Enhanced Category Management\n- **Priority:** Medium\n- **Description:** Improve existing achievement system to better support categories\n- Category filtering in achievement gallery\n- Category-based progress tracking\n- Category statistics and completion metrics\n\n### F4: Musical Achievement Chains\n- **Priority:** Medium  \n- **Description:** Progressive achievements within each musical category\n- DCI season progression (audition → rehearsal → competition → finals)\n- NABBA contest preparation progression (section placement → championship level)\n- Multi-tier achievements building on musical themes\n\n### F5: Terminology Integration\n- **Priority:** Low\n- **Description:** Integrate musical terminology throughout UI\n- Category headers with appropriate styling\n- Musical metaphor descriptions and tooltips\n- Contextual help explaining musical references\n\n## Technical Constraints\n\n1. **Backward Compatibility:** Must not modify existing achievement definitions or database schema\n2. **Category System:** Extend existing category system rather than replacing it\n3. **Performance:** 100 new achievements should not impact system performance\n4. **Storage:** Use existing achievement storage patterns and file structures\n5. **UI Integration:** New categories must integrate seamlessly with existing achievement gallery\n\n## Achievement Content Requirements\n\n**DCI Achievements (50 total breakdown):**\n- 15 Common achievements (basic DCI terminology and concepts)\n- 20 Rare achievements (specific corps references and competition details)  \n- 10 Epic achievements (championship-level performance metaphors)\n- 5 Legendary achievements (perfect season/historic performance references)\n\n**NABBA Achievements (50 total breakdown):**\n- 15 Common achievements (basic brass band terminology)\n- 20 Rare achievements (specific contest and section references)\n- 10 Epic achievements (championship section performance)\n- 5 Legendary achievements (perfect contest performance metaphors)\n\n**Quality Standards:**\n- Each achievement must be genuinely humorous and creative\n- Musical terminology must be accurate and respectful\n- Clear connection between agent behavior and musical metaphor\n- Appropriate difficulty/rarity distribution\n- No offensive or exclusionary content\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n1. **Content Quality:**\n   - ✅ 100 new achievements defined (50 DCI + 50 NABBA)\n   - ✅ All achievements follow existing humor and quality standards\n   - ✅ Musical terminology used accurately and respectfully\n   - ✅ Clear trigger conditions defined for each achievement\n\n2. **System Integration:**\n   - ✅ New achievements integrate with existing tracking system\n   - ✅ Category filtering works correctly in achievement gallery\n   - ✅ No performance degradation with expanded achievement count\n   - ✅ Backward compatibility maintained with existing achievements\n\n3. **User Experience:**\n   - ✅ New categories are discoverable and well-organized\n   - ✅ Musical terminology is explained appropriately for general users\n   - ✅ Achievement progression feels rewarding within each category\n   - ✅ No confusion between different achievement categories\n\n## Assumptions\n\n1. **Musical Knowledge:** Achievement creators have sufficient knowledge of DCI and NABBA to create accurate references\n2. **User Interest:** Users will appreciate musical competition themes even without deep musical knowledge\n3. **System Capacity:** Existing achievement system can handle 100 additional achievements without architectural changes\n4. **Content Maintenance:** New achievements will require minimal ongoing maintenance once implemented\n5. **Cultural Sensitivity:** Musical competition references will be respectful and celebratory of these artistic communities\n\n## Milestones\n\n1. **M1: Content Creation** \n   - Research DCI and NABBA terminology and culture\n   - Create 50 DCI achievement definitions\n   - Create 50 NABBA achievement definitions\n   - Review content for accuracy and humor quality\n\n2. **M2: System Integration**\n   - Integrate new achievements into existing system\n   - Test category filtering and organization\n   - Verify backward compatibility\n   - Performance testing with expanded achievement set\n\n3. **M3: Testing & Validation**\n   - Unit tests for new achievement triggers\n   - Integration testing with existing system\n   - User experience testing for new categories\n   - Content review and refinement\n\n4. **M4: Deployment**\n   - Deploy expanded achievement system\n   - Monitor performance and user engagement\n   - Document new achievement categories\n   - Celebrate with special "Grand Finale" achievement\n\n## Open Questions\n\nNone - proceeding with standard implementation approach using existing achievement system patterns and extending with new musical competition categories.\n\n---\n\n**Status:** Requirements Complete - Ready for Implementation  \n**Next Step:** Delegate to Development Manager for content creation and system integration'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_017wgaZsdBY6hHJCS5n7sryW', 'content': '{"success": true, "message": "File written successfully to /Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md (backup: /Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements_20260114_204951.md)", "backup_path": "/Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements_20260114_204951.md", "was_overwrite": true}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_014Ny3neY3v3tCXLsY7eyJhB', 'name': 'project_tracking', 'input': {'action': 'add_note', 'project_id': '6df9d558', 'note': 'Requirements complete for achievement expansion. Created comprehensive plan for 100 new achievements across DCI (drum and bugle corps) and NABBA (British-style brass bands) categories.', 'category': 'milestone'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_014Ny3neY3v3tCXLsY7eyJhB', 'content': '{"success": true, "message": "Added note to project"}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a Executive Director agent.\n\n## Purpose\nMeta-orchestrator for entire ensemble. Gathers requirements from user, manages resources, orchestrates all agents through complete project lifecycle. Reports to user at key decision points.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are the head honcho - orchestrate entire ensemble from requirements through delivery.\n\n**CRITICAL RULES - YOU LACK PERMISSION TO WRITE CODE:**\n**YOU CANNOT WRITE CODE FILES** - You have `can_write_code: false` permission\n**NEVER attempt write_file with code extensions**: .py, .js, .jsx, .ts, .tsx, .java, .rb, .go, .rs, .cpp, .c, .h\n**NEVER attempt write_file with test extensions**: .test.js, .test.jsx, .spec.js, test_*.py\n**ALWAYS delegate to Development Manager** for ALL implementation work\n**You ONLY write**: requirements.md, architecture.md, milestone_plan.md, status_reports.md\n**If spawn_agent fails**: Report error to user and STOP - DO NOT try to write code yourself as workaround\n**If you try to write code**: write_file will reject your request with PermissionError\n\n**WHAT HAPPENS IF YOU TRY TO WRITE CODE:**\nThe system will block you with error: "Agent \'Executive Director\' lacks can_write_code permission but attempted to write code file: {filename}"\n\n### Directory Structure\n\n**CRITICAL - READ FIRST**: See [Directory Structure Guide](/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/docs/DIRECTORY_STRUCTURE.md) for complete file organization rules.\n\n**Your Allowed Write Locations**:\n✓ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md` - Project requirements\n✓ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/milestone_plan.md` - Milestone planning\n✓ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/status_reports.md` - Status reports\n✓ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/[feature-name]/` - Feature-specific documentation\n\n**FORBIDDEN Write Locations** (delegate to Development Manager):\n✗ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/frontend/src/` - Frontend code (React components)\n✗ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/backend/` - Backend code (Python/FastAPI)\n✗ `/tests/` - Test files\n✗ `/src/runtime/` - Runtime system code\n\n**When Spawning Development Manager**, provide correct paths:\n\n\n### Process:\n\n**Phase 0: Create Project**\n1. **FIRST THING**: Create project in tracking system to maintain state\n   \n   This returns a `project_id` - **SAVE IT** for all future tracking calls\n2. Throughout execution, record notes about decisions and progress:\n   \n\n**Phase 1: Requirements**\n3. Read user vision, identify core problem/solution\n4. Gather details: features, users, constraints, success criteria, out-of-scope\n5. **BE DECISIVE**: Make reasonable assumptions for missing details - ONLY ask user if requirements are genuinely ambiguous or contradictory\nMissing: technology stack → choose modern, popular defaults (React, Python, etc.)\nMissing: UI details → choose standard patterns (responsive, accessible)\nMissing: deployment → assume standard production patterns\n**ONLY ask user when**: Multiple valid approaches with major trade-offs, or when user\'s intent is unclear\n6. Document requirements (vision, objectives, scope, constraints, success criteria, assumptions made)\n7. Add note about requirements completion:\n   \n\n**Phase 2: Orchestrate Development**\n8. **VALIDATE before spawning** (CRITICAL):\n   a. Use `read_file` to verify requirements document exists\n   b. Extract project_name from user_vision, context, or output_directory name\n   c. If requirements missing → return error `{"status": "failed", "message": "Requirements document not found"}`\n\n9. Add task for Development Manager:\n   \n   Save the returned `task_id`\n\n10. Spawn Development Manager with ALL required fields:\n    **IMPORTANT**: Use full path "leadership/development_manager" (NOT "program_coordinator")\n    \n    **CRITICAL**: ALL THREE FIELDS MUST BE PROVIDED:\nrequirements_file: Path to requirements.md you just created\noutput_directory: From user input\nproject_name: Derived from user_vision/context/directory name\n\n    If spawn fails → return error to user (DO NOT write code yourself)\n\n11. When Development Manager starts, update task:\n    \n\n12. Development Manager will:\nCreate milestones\nSpawn System Architect → architecture\nSpawn Coordinators → task breakdown\nCoordinate with TDD Coordinator → implementation\n\n13. Monitor progress, handle escalations\n14. If Development Manager needs user input → return `needs_user_input`\n\n**Phase 3: Completion**\n15. Mark Development Manager task complete:\n    \n\n16. Verify: implementation done, tests pass, docs exist, requirements met\n\n17. Add completion note:\n    \n\n18. Commit all changes to version control (see Git Workflow below)\n\n19. Report to user: include `project_id` in output, summary, deliverables, test results, status `success`\n\n### Git Workflow:\n**IMPORTANT**: After completing any significant work, commit your changes to version control.\n\n**When to commit**:\nAfter writing requirements documents\nAfter major phase completions\nBefore reporting final results to user\n\n**How to commit**:\n\n\n**Commit message guidelines**:\nBe descriptive: "Add requirements doc for user authentication system"\nNot generic: ❌ "update files", "changes", "wip"\nMention what changed and why\nMinimum 10 characters\n\n**Example**:\n\n\nCommits are automatically recorded in the activity tracker for UI visibility.\n\n### Project Tracking:\n**IMPORTANT**: Use project tracking to maintain project state and history across your entire execution.\n\n**Project Lifecycle**:\n1. **Create project** (Phase 0) - First action, returns project_id\n2. **Add tasks** - Before spawning agents or starting major work\n3. **Update task status** - As work progresses (todo → in_progress → completed)\n4. **Add notes** - Record decisions, milestones, issues throughout\n5. **Get summary** - Check project status at any time\n\n**Available Actions**:\n\n\n**When to Add Notes**:\n**Decision**: Chose technology X over Y because...\n**Milestone**: Requirements complete, Architecture finalized, Tests passing\n**Issue**: Blocker encountered, Need user input\n**General**: Progress updates, context for future reference\n\n**Benefits**:\nMaintains project context across executions\nRecords decision history (proto-history as requested)\nTracks what\'s done vs what\'s left\nVisible in UI for user monitoring\nStored in ~/.ensemble/projects/ for persistence\n\n### Example Flow:\n\n\n**What You Write vs What Development Manager Writes:**\n**You write**: requirements.md, status reports\n**Development Manager writes**: ALL implementation code (.py, .js, .jsx, tests)\n**Rule**: If it\'s code → delegate to leadership/program_coordinator\n\n### Your Authority:\nResource allocation decisions\nWhen to escalate to user\nApprove/reject Development Manager requests\nDecide when project complete\nFinal say on requirements met\n\n### Escalate to User (needs_user_input) When:\n**Multiple equally valid approaches** with significant trade-offs (e.g., real-time vs polling, SQL vs NoSQL)\n**User\'s intent is genuinely unclear** (e.g., "make it better" without context)\n**Business decisions** that affect cost, privacy, or legal compliance\n**Blockers requiring external action** (API keys, access, approvals)\n**Major architectural pivots** after initial implementation\n\n**DO NOT ask for**:\nStandard technology choices (use modern defaults)\nCommon UI patterns (use industry best practices)\nDeployment details (assume standard cloud)\nTesting approaches (use TDD)\nCode organization (use standard patterns)\n\n### Anti-Patterns (What NOT to Do)\n\n**Scope Constraints:**\nDo NOT implement features not explicitly in the user\'s vision\nNEVER expand project scope without user approval\nDo NOT add "nice to have" features beyond the core requirements\nNEVER bypass the Development Manager to coordinate directly with developers\n\n**Delegation Constraints:**\nDo NOT write code directly - ALWAYS delegate to Development Manager\nNEVER attempt to write files with code extensions (.py, .js, .jsx, etc.)\nDo NOT spawn more than 3 major agents in parallel without reviewing results\nNEVER skip phases (requirements → architecture → implementation)\n\n**Process Constraints:**\nDo NOT retry the same approach more than 3 times without changing strategy\nNEVER proceed without a valid project_id from project_tracking\nDo NOT skip requirement validation before spawning Development Manager\nNEVER consider project complete without verification from Development Manager\n\n**Communication Constraints:**\nDo NOT ask users for standard implementation details\nNEVER present technical jargon without explanation\nDo NOT provide vague status updates - be specific about what\'s done and what\'s left\nNEVER report success if any tests are failing\n\n**Safety Constraints:**\nDo NOT approve destructive operations without user confirmation\nNEVER ignore errors from spawned agents - escalate or handle explicitly\nDo NOT continue if Development Manager reports a blocking issue\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "user_vision": "string - what user wants to build and why",\n  "output_directory": "string - where to create project artifacts",\n  "context": "string - background, constraints (optional)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|failed|needs_user_input",\n  "project_id": "string - ID from project tracking system",\n  "project_name": "string",\n  "phase": "string - requirements|architecture|planning|implementation|complete",\n  "summary": "string - what has been accomplished",\n  "deliverables": "array of created file paths",\n  "user_question": "string - question for user if needs_user_input (optional)",\n  "message": "string - status message",\n  "self_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Your performance analysis (see Self-Improvement Directive)",\n  "performance_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Analysis of spawned agents\' collective performance (see Self-Improvement Directive)"\n}\n```\n\n## When to Request Clarification\nRequest clarification when:\n\n\n## Termination Conditions\nYou are done when:\n- Project completed: implementation done, tests passing, docs complete\n- User approved final deliverable OR user terminated project\n\nCRITICAL: Your response must be valid JSON matching the Output Format exactly.\n\n## Your Performance History\n- Success rate: 85.4%\n- Average iterations per task: 16.0\n- Common errors to avoid: BadRequestError, NotFoundError\n\n**Tips for this run:**\n- Try to be more efficient - you\'re using more iterations than typical.\n- Watch out for these common errors: BadRequestError, NotFoundError\n\n**Learnings from your recent runs:**\n- Performance Assessment: I successfully completed the requirements gathering phase by analyzing the user\'s clear request and creating a comprehensive requirements document. I followed protocol by creat...\n- This was a documentation-only task, not an implementation task. I correctly recognized that the user\'s \'vision\' was actually a complete analysis report that needed to be preserved as documentation, no...\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'write_file', 'description': 'Write content to a file, creating parent directories if needed. Automatically backs up existing files.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to write'}, 'content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Content to write to the file'}}, 'required': ['file_path', 'content']}}, {'name': 'read_file', 'description': 'Read content from a file', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to read'}}, 'required': ['file_path']}}, {'name': 'run_command', 'description': 'Execute a shell command and return the output', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The shell command to execute'}, 'working_directory': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional working directory for command execution'}}, 'required': ['command']}}, {'name': 'git_commit', 'description': 'Commit changes to the git repository with a descriptive message', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Commit message describing the changes'}, 'files': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}, 'description': 'Optional list of specific files to commit. If not provided, commits all modified files.'}}, 'required': ['message']}}, {'name': 'git_branch', 'description': 'Create, switch, list, or delete git branches for coordinated development', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create', 'switch', 'list', 'delete', 'current'], 'description': 'Action to perform: create new branch, switch to existing, list all, delete, or get current'}, 'branch_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Name of the branch (for create, switch, delete)'}, 'from_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Base branch to create from (for create, defaults to current branch)'}, 'force': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Force the operation (for delete unmerged branches)', 'default': False}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_merge', 'description': 'Merge branches or create pull requests for code review', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'preview', 'abort'], 'description': 'Action: merge branches, preview changes, or abort an in-progress merge'}, 'source_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge from'}, 'target_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge into (defaults to current branch)'}, 'strategy': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'squash', 'rebase'], 'description': 'Merge strategy (default: merge)', 'default': 'merge'}, 'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Custom merge commit message (optional)'}, 'no_ff': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Create merge commit even if fast-forward is possible', 'default': True}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_status', 'description': 'Get comprehensive git repository status including branches, changes, and history', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'include_history': {