es (adjectives, nouns, descriptors)\\n   - Ensure family-friendly content only\\n\\n2. **Name Components**\\n   - Whimsical adjectives (Whimsy, Citrusy, Sparkly, Dreamy, etc.)\\n   - Nature/fantasy nouns (Cloud, Edge, Storm, Meadow, etc.)  \\n   - Action/descriptor words (Strider, Walker, Keeper, Singer, etc.)\\n\\n3. **User Interface**\\n   - Simple button to generate new name\\n   - Display current generated name prominently\\n   - Option to regenerate if user doesn\'t like current name\\n   - Copy-to-clipboard functionality\\n\\n4. **Name Quality**\\n   - All names must be family-friendly\\n   - Names should be memorable and fun\\n   - Avoid repetitive or boring combinations\\n   - 2-3 word combinations for good flow\\n\\n### Technical Requirements\\n1. **Frontend (React)**\\n   - Responsive design that works on all devices\\n   - Clean, playful UI design\\n   - Smooth animations for name generation\\n   - Accessible controls and text\\n\\n2. **Name Generation Logic**\\n   - Client-side generation for instant response\\n   - Large word pools to ensure variety\\n   - Smart combination logic to avoid awkward phrings\\n   - Randomization that feels truly random to users\\n\\n## Non-Functional Requirements\\n\\n### Performance\\n- Instant name generation (< 100ms)\\n- Lightweight implementation\\n- No external API dependencies needed\\n\\n### Usability\\n- One-click name generation\\n- Clear, readable typography\\n- Intuitive interface requiring no instructions\\n\\n### Content Standards\\n- All generated names must be appropriate for all ages\\n- No offensive, scary, or inappropriate word combinations\\n- Positive, uplifting tone in all generated content\\n\\n## User Stories\\n\\n1. **As a user**, I want to click a button and instantly get a fun, whimsical name so I can replace boring default names.\\n\\n2. **As a user**, I want to regenerate names until I find one I like so I can get the perfect name for my needs.\\n\\n3. **As a user**, I want to easily copy the generated name so I can use it elsewhere.\\n\\n4. **As a parent**, I want all generated names to be family-friendly so I can safely use this with my children.\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n\\n1. **Functionality**: Generate unique, whimsical names consistently\\n2. **Quality**: All names are family-friendly and creative\\n3. **Usability**: Users can generate and use names in under 30 seconds\\n4. **Variety**: User sees different names on each generation attempt\\n5. **Performance**: Name generation happens instantly without delays\\n\\n## Scope & Constraints\\n\\n### In Scope\\n- Single-page application with name generation\\n- Client-side name generation\\n- Basic styling and responsive design\\n- Copy-to-clipboard functionality\\n\\n### Out of Scope\\n- User accounts or name saving\\n- Name history or favorites\\n- Social sharing features\\n- Custom word pool management\\n- Backend storage or APIs\\n\\n### Technical Assumptions\\n- Modern web browser support (ES6+)\\n- Client-side only implementation\\n- React with standard tooling\\n- No external dependencies for name generation\\n\\n### Content Assumptions\\n- Pre-curated word lists ensure quality\\n- English language only\\n- PG-rated content standards\\n- 50+ words per category for variety\\n\\n## Implementation Priority\\n\\n### Phase 1: Core Generation\\n1. Create word pools (adjectives, nouns, descriptors)\\n2. Implement random combination logic\\n3. Basic React component for generation\\n\\n### Phase 2: User Interface\\n1. Clean, playful UI design\\n2. Generate button and name display\\n3. Copy-to-clipboard functionality\\n\\n### Phase 3: Polish\\n1. Responsive design\\n2. Smooth animations\\n3. Accessibility improvements\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\nThe name generator is complete when:\\n- [ ] Users can generate whimsical names with one click\\n- [ ] All generated names are family-friendly and creative\\n- [ ] Names have good variety and don\'t feel repetitive\\n- [ ] UI is clean, responsive, and easy to use\\n- [ ] Copy-to-clipboard works on all major browsers\\n- [ ] No offensive or inappropriate names can be generated\\n- [ ] Performance is instant (< 100ms generation time)"}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_01B9qXKi5WKEsGrxVYjktwvL', 'name': 'write_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md', 'content': "# Whimsical Name Generator - Architecture Proposal\n\n## Architecture Overview\n\n### High-Level Design\nThe Whimsical Name Generator will be implemented as a **Single-Page Application (SPA)** using a **Component-Based Architecture** pattern. This approach provides:\n\n- **Simplicity**: No backend required - all logic runs client-side\n- **Performance**: Instant name generation without network requests\n- **Maintainability**: Clear separation of concerns between UI and business logic\n- **Scalability**: Easy to extend with new word categories or features\n\n### Architecture Pattern: Model-View-Controller (MVC)\n- **Model**: Word pools and name generation logic\n- **View**: React components for UI rendering\n- **Controller**: React hooks and event handlers for user interactions\n\n**Rationale**: MVC provides clear separation of data, presentation, and interaction logic, making the codebase maintainable and testable.\n\n## Tech Stack\n\n### Frontend Framework: **React 18** with TypeScript\n**Why React**:\n- Excellent component reusability and composition\n- Rich ecosystem and tooling\n- Great developer experience with hot reloading\n- Strong TypeScript integration for better code quality\n- Industry standard for interactive UIs\n\n**Why TypeScript**:\n- Compile-time error checking prevents runtime bugs\n- Better IDE support with autocomplete and refactoring\n- Self-documenting code with type definitions\n- Easier team collaboration and maintenance\n\n**Alternatives Considered**:\n- Vue.js: Good choice but React has larger ecosystem\n- Vanilla JavaScript: Would work but lacks structure for growth\n\n### Build Tool: **Vite**\n**Why Vite**:\n- Extremely fast development server with HMR\n- Modern ES modules support\n- Excellent TypeScript support out of the box\n- Much faster than Create React App\n- Simple configuration\n\n### Styling: **CSS Modules + Vanilla CSS**\n**Why CSS Modules**:\n- Scoped styles prevent naming conflicts\n- Explicit dependencies between components and styles\n- No runtime overhead like styled-components\n- Easy to understand and debug\n\n**Why Not CSS-in-JS**:\n- Adds complexity and bundle size for a simple app\n- CSS Modules provide sufficient isolation\n\n### State Management: **React useState + useReducer**\n**Why Built-in Hooks**:\n- Simple application state doesn't require Redux complexity\n- Fewer dependencies and smaller bundle size\n- Easy to understand and maintain\n\n### Development Tools:\n- **ESLint + Prettier**: Code quality and formatting\n- **Jest + React Testing Library**: Unit and integration testing\n- **Storybook**: Component development and documentation\n\n## System Components\n\n### 1. Name Generation Engine (`NameGenerator`)\n**Responsibilities**:\n- Store word pools (adjectives, nouns, descriptors)\n- Implement combination algorithms\n- Ensure quality and variety in generated names\n- Provide filtering logic for family-friendly content\n\n**Core Methods**:\n```typescript\ninterface NameGenerator {\n  generateName(): string;\n  getWordPools(): WordPools;\n  validateName(name: string): boolean;\n}\n```\n\n### 2. UI Components\n\n#### App Component (`App`)\n- Root component managing global state\n- Coordinates between child components\n- Handles theme and global settings\n\n#### Name Display (`NameDisplay`)\n- Renders the currently generated name\n- Handles text formatting and styling\n- Manages copy-to-clipboard functionality\n\n#### Generation Controls (`GenerateButton`)\n- Trigger button for name generation\n- Loading states and feedback\n- Keyboard accessibility support\n\n#### Animation Layer (`AnimationWrapper`)\n- Smooth transitions between name changes\n- Fun visual effects during generation\n- Performance-optimized animations\n\n### 3. Utilities\n\n#### Word Pool Manager (`WordPoolManager`)\n- Curates and validates word lists\n- Provides category-based word access\n- Handles word pool expansion\n\n#### Clipboard Helper (`ClipboardUtil`)\n- Cross-browser clipboard API integration\n- Fallback for older browsers\n- User feedback for copy operations\n\n## Data Flow\n\n```\nUser clicks Generate Button\n    ↓\nGenerateButton triggers onGenerate\n    ↓\nApp calls NameGenerator.generateName()\n    ↓\nNameGenerator selects random words from pools\n    ↓\nNameGenerator combines words using algorithm\n    ↓\nGenerated name passed to NameDisplay\n    ↓\nNameDisplay renders with animation\n    ↓\nUser can copy name to clipboard\n```\n\n## File/Directory Structure\n\n```\nwhimsical-name-generator/\n├── public/\n│   ├── index.html\n│   └── favicon.ico\n├── src/\n│   ├── components/\n│   │   ├── App/\n│   │   │   ├── App.tsx\n│   │   │   ├── App.module.css\n│   │   │   └── App.test.tsx\n│   │   ├── NameDisplay/\n│   │   │   ├── NameDisplay.tsx\n│   │   │   ├── NameDisplay.module.css\n│   │   │   └── NameDisplay.test.tsx\n│   │   ├── GenerateButton/\n│   │   │   ├── GenerateButton.tsx\n│   │   │   ├── GenerateButton.module.css\n│   │   │   └── GenerateButton.test.tsx\n│   │   └── AnimationWrapper/\n│   │       ├── AnimationWrapper.tsx\n│   │       └── AnimationWrapper.module.css\n│   ├── services/\n│   │   ├── NameGenerator.ts\n│   │   └── NameGenerator.test.ts\n│   ├── data/\n│   │   ├── wordPools.ts\n│   │   └── wordPools.test.ts\n│   ├── utils/\n│   │   ├── clipboard.ts\n│   │   ├── clipboard.test.ts\n│   │   └── constants.ts\n│   ├── types/\n│   │   └── index.ts\n│   ├── styles/\n│   │   ├── globals.css\n│   │   └── variables.css\n│   ├── main.tsx\n│   └── vite-env.d.ts\n├── .storybook/\n│   ├── main.ts\n│   └── preview.ts\n├── package.json\n├── vite.config.ts\n├── tsconfig.json\n├── .eslintrc.js\n└── README.md\n```\n\n## Data Model\n\n### Word Pool Structure\n```typescript\ninterface WordPools {\n  adjectives: string[];\n  nouns: string[];\n  descriptors: string[];\n}\n\ninterface GeneratedName {\n  full: string;\n  parts: {\n    adjective?: string;\n    noun: string;\n    descriptor?: string;\n  };\n  timestamp: Date;\n}\n```\n\n### Application State\n```typescript\ninterface AppState {\n  currentName: GeneratedName | null;\n  isGenerating: boolean;\n  copyStatus: 'idle' | 'copying' | 'copied' | 'error';\n  nameHistory: GeneratedName[]; // For potential future features\n}\n```\n\n## Name Generation Algorithm\n\n### Strategy: **Weighted Random Selection**\n1. **Word Pool Selection**: Randomly select from each category with equal probability\n2. **Pattern Variation**: Alternate between 2-word and 3-word patterns\n3. **Quality Filtering**: Validate combinations for flow and appropriateness\n4. **Repetition Prevention**: Track recent generations to avoid immediate repeats\n\n### Generation Patterns:\n- Pattern A: `[Adjective] + [Noun] + [Descriptor]` (70%)\n- Pattern B: `[Adjective] + [Noun]` (20%)\n- Pattern C: `[Noun] + [Descriptor]` (10%)\n\n### Quality Assurance:\n- Phonetic flow checking (avoid harsh consonant combinations)\n- Semantic validation (ensure positive, whimsical tone)\n- Length constraints (2-4 syllables per word, 15-30 characters total)\n\n## Performance Strategy\n\n### Optimization Techniques:\n1. **Client-Side Generation**: Zero network latency\n2. **Preloaded Word Pools**: All data loaded on app initialization\n3. **Memoized Components**: React.memo for stable components\n4. **Efficient Algorithms**: O(1) random selection from pre-indexed arrays\n5. **Bundle Optimization**: Tree shaking and code splitting\n\n### Performance Targets:\n- Initial load: < 1 second\n- Name generation: < 50ms\n- Animation duration: 300ms\n- Bundle size: < 100KB gzipped\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\n### Unit Testing (Jest + React Testing Library)\n- **Components**: Render correctly, handle user interactions\n- **Services**: Name generation logic, word pool validation\n- **Utils**: Clipboard functionality, helper functions\n\n### Integration Testing\n- **User Flows**: Complete name generation and copying workflow\n- **Component Integration**: Proper communication between components\n- **State Management**: State updates and side effects\n\n### Accessibility Testing\n- **Keyboard Navigation**: Full keyboard accessibility\n- **Screen Reader**: ARIA labels and semantic HTML\n- **Color Contrast**: WCAG AA compliance\n\n### Performance Testing\n- **Bundle Analysis**: Size and load time monitoring\n- **Memory Leaks**: Component cleanup verification\n- **Animation Performance**: 60fps target maintenance\n\n## Deployment Strategy\n\n### Development Environment\n- **Local Development**: Vite dev server with hot reload\n- **Preview Builds**: Vite preview for production-like testing\n- **Storybook**: Component development and documentation\n\n### Production Deployment\n**Platform**: **Vercel** (recommended) or **Netlify**\n\n**Why Vercel**:\n- Excellent React/Vite integration\n- Automatic deployments from Git\n- Edge network for global performance\n- Zero configuration required\n- Free tier sufficient for this project\n\n**Build Process**:\n1. `npm run build` - Vite production build\n2. Automatic optimization (minification, tree shaking)\n3. Static asset generation\n4. Deploy to CDN\n\n### CI/CD Pipeline\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/deploy.yml\n- Run tests and linting\n- Build production bundle\n- Deploy to Vercel\n- Run smoke tests on deployed version\n```\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n### Framework Alternatives\n**Vue.js**: \n- **Pros**: Simpler learning curve, excellent documentation\n- **Cons**: Smaller ecosystem, less industry adoption\n- **Decision**: React chosen for ecosystem and team familiarity\n\n**Svelte**:\n- **Pros**: Smaller bundle size, compile-time optimizations\n- **Cons**: Smaller community, fewer resources\n- **Decision**: Too niche for mainstream project\n\n### State Management Alternatives\n**Redux Toolkit**:\n- **Pros**: Predictable state updates, time-travel debugging\n- **Cons**: Adds complexity for simple state needs\n- **Decision**: Built-in hooks sufficient for current scope\n\n### Deployment Alternatives\n**AWS S3 + CloudFront**:\n- **Pros**: More control, potentially lower costs at scale\n- **Cons**: More complex setup, requires AWS knowledge\n- **Decision**: Vercel chosen for simplicity and developer experience\n\n## Risks and Mitigations\n\n### Risk 1: Limited Word Pool Variety\n**Impact**: Users see repetitive names\n**Mitigation**: \n- Start with 100+ words per category\n- Implement smart combination logic\n- Plan for easy word pool expansion\n\n### Risk 2: Inappropriate Name Combinations\n**Impact**: Family-friendly requirement violated\n**Mitigation**:\n- Pre-curated word lists only\n- Combination validation logic\n- Comprehensive testing of all possible combinations\n\n### Risk 3: Browser Compatibility Issues\n**Impact**: Copy-to-clipboard fails on older browsers\n**Mitigation**:\n- Feature detection and graceful fallbacks\n- Alternative copy methods (select text)\n- Clear browser support documentation\n\n### Risk 4: Performance on Low-End Devices\n**Impact**: Slow animations or generation\n**Mitigation**:\n- Performance budget monitoring\n- Reduced motion preferences support\n- Progressive enhancement approach\n\n## Open Questions for User Review\n\n1. **Animation Preferences**: Do you want elaborate animations or subtle transitions for name generation?\n\n2. **Word Pool Size**: Would you prefer a smaller, highly-curated word pool (50-75 words) or larger variety (100+ words)?\n\n3. **Name Patterns**: Should we stick to the proposed patterns or add more variety (compound words, alliteration)?\n\n4. **Theme/Styling**: Any specific visual theme preferences (minimalist, playful, fantasy-themed)?\n\n5. **Future Features**: Priority on name history, favorites, or sharing features for future iterations?\n\n## Implementation Timeline\n\n### Phase 1: Core Functionality (Week 1)\n- Set up project structure and build tools\n- Implement NameGenerator service\n- Create basic React components\n- Add core word pools (minimum viable set)\n\n### Phase 2: User Interface (Week 2)\n- Polish component styling and responsive design\n- Add animations and transitions\n- Implement copy-to-clipboard\n- Comprehensive testing suite\n\n### Phase 3: Deployment & Polish (Week 3)\n- Production deployment setup\n- Performance optimization\n- Accessibility improvements\n- Documentation and final testing\n\n**Total Estimated Timeline**: 3 weeks for full implementation and deployment.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThis architecture provides a solid foundation for the Whimsical Name Generator that meets all requirements while remaining simple, maintainable, and extensible. The client-side approach ensures instant performance, while the component-based structure allows for easy feature additions in the future.\n\nThe proposed tech stack leverages modern, well-supported technologies that provide excellent developer experience and long-term maintainability. The emphasis on testing and quality assurance ensures the family-friendly requirement is consistently met."}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_01B9qXKi5WKEsGrxVYjktwvL', 'content': '{"success": true, "message": "File written successfully to /Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md (backup: /Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture_20260114_205838.md)", "backup_path": "/Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture_20260114_205838.md", "was_overwrite": true}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Return Key Decisions**: Highlight the most important architectural decisions for user review\n\n### Guidelines:\n**Justify all decisions** - don\'t just choose tech, explain why\n**Consider alternatives** - show you\'ve thought through options\n**Be specific** - provide concrete details, not just buzzwords\n**Think about maintainability** - code will need to be updated\n**Consider the full lifecycle** - development, testing, deployment, monitoring\n**Match complexity to need** - don\'t over-engineer, but don\'t under-design\n**Highlight trade-offs** - be honest about pros and cons\n**Make it reviewable** - user needs to understand and approve this\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "requirements_file": "Path to requirements document to base architecture on",\n  "output_file": "Path where architecture proposal should be written",\n  "constraints": "Optional: Specific technical constraints (e.g., must use Python, deploy on AWS)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|needs_clarification",\n  "architecture_file": "Path to the written architecture proposal",\n  "message": "Summary of architectural approach",\n  "key_decisions": ["List of major architectural decisions for user review"],\n  "self_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Your performance analysis (2-4 sentences, see Self-Improvement Directive)"\n}\n```\n\n## When to Request Clarification\nRequest clarification when:\n\n\n## Termination Conditions\nYou are done when:\n- Complete architecture proposal has been written\n- Tech stack is justified with trade-offs explained\n- File structure and component breakdown are defined\n- Data flow is documented\n- Alternative approaches are considered\n\nCRITICAL: Your response must be valid JSON matching the Output Format exactly.\n\n## Your Performance History\n- Success rate: 100.0%\n- Average iterations per task: 3.5\n\n**Tips for this run:**\n- Great work! Maintain your high performance standards.\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'write_file', 'description': 'Write content to a file, creating parent directories if needed. Automatically backs up existing files.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to write'}, 'content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Content to write to the file'}}, 'required': ['file_path', 'content']}}, {'name': 'read_file', 'description': 'Read content from a file', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to read'}}, 'required': ['file_path']}}, {'name': 'run_command', 'description': 'Execute a shell command and return the output', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The shell command to execute'}, 'working_directory': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional working directory for command execution'}}, 'required': ['command']}}, {'name': 'git_commit', 'description': 'Commit changes to the git repository with a descriptive message', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Commit message describing the changes'}, 'files': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}, 'description': 'Optional list of specific files to commit. If not provided, commits all modified files.'}}, 'required': ['message']}}, {'name': 'git_branch', 'description': 'Create, switch, list, or delete git branches for coordinated development', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create', 'switch', 'list', 'delete', 'current'], 'description': 'Action to perform: create new branch, switch to existing, list all, delete, or get current'}, 'branch_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Name of the branch (for create, switch, delete)'}, 'from_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Base branch to create from (for create, defaults to current branch)'}, 'force': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Force the operation (for delete unmerged branches)', 'default': False}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_merge', 'description': 'Merge branches or create pull requests for code review', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'preview', 'abort'], 'description': 'Action: merge branches, preview changes, or abort an in-progress merge'}, 'source_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge from'}, 'target_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge into (defaults to current branch)'}, 'strategy': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'squash', 'rebase'], 'description': 'Merge strategy (default: merge)', 'default': 'merge'}, 'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Custom merge commit message (optional)'}, 'no_ff': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Create merge commit even if fast-forward is possible', 'default': True}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_status', 'description': 'Get comprehensive git repository status including branches, changes, and history', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'include_history': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Include recent commit history', 'default': False}, 'history_count': {'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Number of commits to include in history', 'default': 10}}, 'required': []}}, {'name': 'project_tracking', 'description': 'Manage project state, tasks, and notes for the current project', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create_project', 'add_task', 'update_task', 'add_note', 'get_summary', 'get_next_tasks'], 'description': 'Action to perform'}, 'project_id': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Project ID (not needed for create_project)'}, 'project_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Project name (for create_project)'}, 'description': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Description (for create_project or add_task)'}, 'task_id': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Task ID (for update_task)'}, 'status': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['todo', 'in_progress', 'completed', 'blocked', 'cancelled'], 'description': 'Task status (for update_task)'}, 'note': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Note text (for add_note or update_task)'}, 'assigned_to': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Agent type (for add_task)'}, 'category': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['general', 'decision', 'milestone', 'issue'], 'description': 'Note category (for add_note)'}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'spawn_agent', 'description': 'Spawn and execute a specialist agent to perform a specific task', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'agent_type': {'type': 'string', 'description': "The type of agent to spawn (e.g., 'code_writer', 'code_tester')"}, 'input_data': {'type': 'object', 'description': 'Input data to pass to the spawned agent'}}, 'required': ['agent_type', 'input_data']}}]}}"}
