 generator that combines creative adjectives, nouns, and descriptors to create unique, family-friendly names on demand.\\n\\n## Functional Requirements\\n\\n### Core Features\\n1. **Random Name Generation**\\n   - Generate unique whimsical names on demand\\n   - Combine multiple word categories (adjectives, nouns, descriptors)\\n   - Ensure family-friendly content only\\n\\n2. **Name Components**\\n   - Whimsical adjectives (Whimsy, Citrusy, Sparkly, Dreamy, etc.)\\n   - Nature/fantasy nouns (Cloud, Edge, Storm, Meadow, etc.)  \\n   - Action/descriptor words (Strider, Walker, Keeper, Singer, etc.)\\n\\n3. **User Interface**\\n   - Simple button to generate new name\\n   - Display current generated name prominently\\n   - Option to regenerate if user doesn\'t like current name\\n   - Copy-to-clipboard functionality\\n\\n4. **Name Quality**\\n   - All names must be family-friendly\\n   - Names should be memorable and fun\\n   - Avoid repetitive or boring combinations\\n   - 2-3 word combinations for good flow\\n\\n### Technical Requirements\\n1. **Frontend (React)**\\n   - Responsive design that works on all devices\\n   - Clean, playful UI design\\n   - Smooth animations for name generation\\n   - Accessible controls and text\\n\\n2. **Name Generation Logic**\\n   - Client-side generation for instant response\\n   - Large word pools to ensure variety\\n   - Smart combination logic to avoid awkward phrings\\n   - Randomization that feels truly random to users\\n\\n## Non-Functional Requirements\\n\\n### Performance\\n- Instant name generation (< 100ms)\\n- Lightweight implementation\\n- No external API dependencies needed\\n\\n### Usability\\n- One-click name generation\\n- Clear, readable typography\\n- Intuitive interface requiring no instructions\\n\\n### Content Standards\\n- All generated names must be appropriate for all ages\\n- No offensive, scary, or inappropriate word combinations\\n- Positive, uplifting tone in all generated content\\n\\n## User Stories\\n\\n1. **As a user**, I want to click a button and instantly get a fun, whimsical name so I can replace boring default names.\\n\\n2. **As a user**, I want to regenerate names until I find one I like so I can get the perfect name for my needs.\\n\\n3. **As a user**, I want to easily copy the generated name so I can use it elsewhere.\\n\\n4. **As a parent**, I want all generated names to be family-friendly so I can safely use this with my children.\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n\\n1. **Functionality**: Generate unique, whimsical names consistently\\n2. **Quality**: All names are family-friendly and creative\\n3. **Usability**: Users can generate and use names in under 30 seconds\\n4. **Variety**: User sees different names on each generation attempt\\n5. **Performance**: Name generation happens instantly without delays\\n\\n## Scope & Constraints\\n\\n### In Scope\\n- Single-page application with name generation\\n- Client-side name generation\\n- Basic styling and responsive design\\n- Copy-to-clipboard functionality\\n\\n### Out of Scope\\n- User accounts or name saving\\n- Name history or favorites\\n- Social sharing features\\n- Custom word pool management\\n- Backend storage or APIs\\n\\n### Technical Assumptions\\n- Modern web browser support (ES6+)\\n- Client-side only implementation\\n- React with standard tooling\\n- No external dependencies for name generation\\n\\n### Content Assumptions\\n- Pre-curated word lists ensure quality\\n- English language only\\n- PG-rated content standards\\n- 50+ words per category for variety\\n\\n## Implementation Priority\\n\\n### Phase 1: Core Generation\\n1. Create word pools (adjectives, nouns, descriptors)\\n2. Implement random combination logic\\n3. Basic React component for generation\\n\\n### Phase 2: User Interface\\n1. Clean, playful UI design\\n2. Generate button and name display\\n3. Copy-to-clipboard functionality\\n\\n### Phase 3: Polish\\n1. Responsive design\\n2. Smooth animations\\n3. Accessibility improvements\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\nThe name generator is complete when:\\n- [ ] Users can generate whimsical names with one click\\n- [ ] All generated names are family-friendly and creative\\n- [ ] Names have good variety and don\'t feel repetitive\\n- [ ] UI is clean, responsive, and easy to use\\n- [ ] Copy-to-clipboard works on all major browsers\\n- [ ] No offensive or inappropriate names can be generated\\n- [ ] Performance is instant (< 100ms generation time)"}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Return Key Decisions**: Highlight the most important architectural decisions for user review\n\n### Guidelines:\n**Justify all decisions** - don\'t just choose tech, explain why\n**Consider alternatives** - show you\'ve thought through options\n**Be specific** - provide concrete details, not just buzzwords\n**Think about maintainability** - code will need to be updated\n**Consider the full lifecycle** - development, testing, deployment, monitoring\n**Match complexity to need** - don\'t over-engineer, but don\'t under-design\n**Highlight trade-offs** - be honest about pros and cons\n**Make it reviewable** - user needs to understand and approve this\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "requirements_file": "Path to requirements document to base architecture on",\n  "output_file": "Path where architecture proposal should be written",\n  "constraints": "Optional: Specific technical constraints (e.g., must use Python, deploy on AWS)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|needs_clarification",\n  "architecture_file": "Path to the written architecture proposal",\n  "message": "Summary of architectural approach",\n  "key_decisions": ["List of major architectural decisions for user review"],\n  "self_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Your performance analysis (2-4 sentences, see Self-Improvement Directive)"\n}\n```\n\n## When to Request Clarification\nRequest clarification when:\n\n\n## Termination Conditions\nYou are done when:\n- Complete architecture proposal has been written\n- Tech stack is justified with trade-offs explained\n- File structure and component breakdown are defined\n- Data flow is documented\n- Alternative approaches are considered\n\nCRITICAL: Your response must be valid JSON matching the Output Format exactly.\n\n## Your Performance History\n- Success rate: 100.0%\n- Average iterations per task: 3.5\n\n**Tips for this run:**\n- Great work! Maintain your high performance standards.\n\n**Learnings from your recent runs:**\n- I delivered a thorough architecture proposal that addresses all requirements with clear justifications for each decision. The design appropriately matches the simplicity of the requirements while prov...\n- Successfully analyzed a straightforward SPA project and made appropriate architectural choices that match the simple requirements. Avoided over-engineering while still providing a robust, maintainable...\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'write_file', 'description': 'Write content to a file, creating parent directories if needed. Automatically backs up existing files.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to write'}, 'content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Content to write to the file'}}, 'required': ['file_path', 'content']}}, {'name': 'read_file', 'description': 'Read content from a file', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to read'}}, 'required': ['file_path']}}, {'name': 'run_command', 'description': 'Execute a shell command and return the output', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The shell command to execute'}, 'working_directory': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional working directory for command execution'}}, 'required': ['command']}}, {'name': 'git_commit', 'description': 'Commit changes to the git repository with a descriptive message', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Commit message describing the changes'}, 'files': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}, 'description': 'Optional list of specific files to commit. If not provided, commits all modified files.'}}, 'required': ['message']}}, {'name': 'git_branch', 'description': 'Create, switch, list, or delete git branches for coordinated development', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create', 'switch', 'list', 'delete', 'current'], 'description': 'Action to perform: create new branch, switch to existing, list all, delete, or get current'}, 'branch_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Name of the branch (for create, switch, delete)'}, 'from_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Base branch to create from (for create, 